WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.122
Bundle adjustment is still powerful, says the slide.

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:05.040
It's VGGT's own table.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.653
Ten frames go from eighty-five point three to ninety-three point five, for

00:00:09.653 --> 00:00:11.576
one point six more seconds.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:14.670
Hi, I'm Fennick.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.190
This week, three of four posts say the same thing: a network

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.536
makes the guess, and an optimiser finishes it.

00:00:22.492 --> 00:00:27.687
Kosmo-GFM predicts cameras, depth, moving points and matches from a set of images.

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:32.589
Then PoseEngine groups images into stars of twenty-six, runs the network on

00:00:32.589 --> 00:00:34.851
each, and finishes with bundle adjustment.

00:00:36.492 --> 00:00:38.176
A three-sixty walk down a room.

00:00:38.176 --> 00:00:41.545
Each frame casts a ray to every wall point, and the true

00:00:41.545 --> 00:00:42.949
rays meet on the walls.

00:00:43.369 --> 00:00:45.232
Turn each heading by two degrees.

00:00:45.232 --> 00:00:49.580
All its rays turn too, and the points land eighteen point three centimetres off.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.746
Bundle adjustment solves headings and points together, from the same rays.

00:00:53.746 --> 00:00:56.470
The error drops to under half a centimetre.

00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:02.858
On thirty-four scenes PoseEngine has the best pose accuracy, in fourteen hours,

00:01:02.858 --> 00:01:04.896
a fifth of GLUEMAP's time.

00:01:06.242 --> 00:01:11.215
But splats trained from its poses trail GLUEMAP's by one point seven

00:01:11.215 --> 00:01:12.872
decibels on Mip-NeRF three-sixty.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:19.312
Its training data, KosmoWorld, renders real scans as pinhole, fisheye and panorama

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:20.960
frames, each with depth.

00:01:23.742 --> 00:01:27.312
The paper reconstructs rooms from fisheye and panoramic frames.

00:01:27.692 --> 00:01:31.661
The release supports pinhole cameras only, and the pose engine isn't in it.

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:37.194
I ran the released models on a desk clip with motion capture.

00:01:37.194 --> 00:01:41.420
Give the camera-conditioned model exact poses and points land twenty-two centimetres off.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.238
Add five degrees of noise and it's thirty-nine.

00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:45.999
It trusts what it's given.

00:01:47.492 --> 00:01:51.712
Budget3D lets one image-to-3D model run on fewer tokens.

00:01:52.092 --> 00:01:54.733
Empty space merges into coarse tokens first.

00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:58.884
At fifty percent, sampling is one point seven nine times faster.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:04.979
Idol's eight thousand one hundred ninety-two tokens sit on a three-level octree.

00:02:05.379 --> 00:02:08.910
The first layout is built from pure noise, with no dense warm-up.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.499
At step five it is rebuilt from the model's own clean estimate.

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:14.381
Then it stays fixed.

00:02:14.381 --> 00:02:15.493
Speed is measured.

00:02:15.493 --> 00:02:16.976
Quality isn't published yet.

00:02:18.742 --> 00:02:22.479
Solv3R's demo streams two hundred eighty-eight frames of a desk.

00:02:22.859 --> 00:02:27.295
The splat training in the clip is a panel the public demo doesn't contain.

00:02:27.295 --> 00:02:28.563
Its COLMAP export is.

00:02:29.892 --> 00:02:33.295
Use the network for the guess, and keep the bundle adjustment.

00:02:33.295 --> 00:02:35.150
What Kosmo released is the guess.

00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:39.783
Network first, optimiser after.

00:02:39.783 --> 00:02:41.070
Thanks for watching!

00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:44.699
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:44.699 --> 00:02:45.280
I'm Fennick.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:45.571
Bye!
